A||||2||Shinerdc||"Help" re flush||||1||
Z||000000||Shinerdc||11-24-2001||11:13 PM||||motor is sitting...waiting to find a head....i just bought it....was run a lot with bad head and oil all over...thick sludgey ect....lots carbon/whatever on the pistons...filling in the sides down to the rings...QUITE CONCERNED...how do i get it to drain right down....spraying engine cleaner ...will engine cleaner damage other gaskets or seals....compressed air and blow it down by....i keep things well coated and puddled with oil.....once i get it going will do a flush...and couple of oil changes...any suggestions....ps whats best way do recondition bl;ock for new head gasket....steel wool? may get some strands in cylinder....could be rough on heads...<P>thanks ..Shiner...rookie mechanic||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||11-25-2001||09:58 AM||||you can soak it with transmission fluid (has alot of conditioners in fluid) and then if you need to scrap it off with a plastic scraper.  To clean the block/head use a gasket scraper or razor blade and finish it off with a "scotch pad".   Hope this helps.<P>Pete||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-25-2001||02:32 PM||||If the head mating surface is crappy, then consider 3M gasket remover disks and/or bristle wheels. These work faster than scraping and will not damage the surface like sanding or scraping can. They will leave the block ready for the new gasket.<P>Also, you do not use sealers on most head gaskets.<P>Stuck on crap in the cylinder can be removed with solvents or tranny fluid or whatever else will soak it off. plastic scrapers and "Scotch-Brite" can also be used.<P>For carbon on pistons... soak them with standard Gumout or other good brand Carb cleaner. Keep them wet for a hour or more and allot of the crap will lift right off. Work them with some Scotch-Brite if needed. This is best done with the piston at the top of the bore. <P>However if there is corosion of the walls or pistons then the block is pretty much trash. It's cheaper to go get another whole motor than redo it. You may be able to scrub off the corosion with scotch brite but the damage is done and will likely cause the motor to smoke or even fail a ring. maybe not right away but it could be very soon there after. There's no way to know.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
